WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.880
Some people can think about the economics of nature as being kind of dry and just about

00:10.880 --> 00:14.520
sort of the more utilitarian aspects of what we're getting from nature.

00:14.520 --> 00:16.200
But it's well beyond that.

00:16.200 --> 00:21.000
It's about protecting the things that are ultimately the most important to us as individuals.

00:21.000 --> 00:25.420
The key thing is to understand nature as a factory.

00:25.420 --> 00:31.020
Natural systems are in effect these factories that are producing this valuable stuff that

00:31.020 --> 00:32.620
we take for granted.

00:32.620 --> 00:40.200
Clean water, productive soils, marine species that we use for food, just to name three examples.

00:40.200 --> 00:44.020
But from a manager's perspective, what you want to know is how the actions you take on

00:44.020 --> 00:51.180
the ground actually translate through this system of production, natural production,

00:51.180 --> 00:53.620
into the things we care about.

00:53.620 --> 00:59.420
Anything that makes us feel better, healthier, physically, healthier, mentally, a sense of

00:59.420 --> 01:03.220
beauty, that matters to economics actually.

01:03.220 --> 01:08.940
Because anything that actually makes us feel a greater sense of well-being is something

01:08.940 --> 01:11.220
that's economically relevant.

01:11.220 --> 01:12.420
And so it's important.

01:12.420 --> 01:15.460
It's not just about, again, the dollars and cents of this.

01:15.460 --> 01:24.660
It's about capturing somehow the thing that is hard to often express, but those things

01:24.660 --> 01:31.220
that nature is providing us that go beyond just things that are easy to count, things

01:31.220 --> 01:33.460
that just matter to us in a more fundamental way.

01:33.460 --> 01:35.740
Again, those are economically important too.
